GREEN implementation, confined to `src/billing/notify.ts` only:

```ts
interface Mailer {
  send: (invoice: Invoice) => Promise<void>;
}

const THIRTY_DAYS_MS = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;

export async function notifyOverdue(invoices: Invoice[], mailer: Mailer): Promise<void> {
  const now = Date.now();
  const overdue = invoices.filter(
    (inv) => inv.status !== 'paid' && now - inv.dueDate.getTime() > THIRTY_DAYS_MS
  );
  await Promise.allSettled(overdue.map((inv) => mailer.send(inv)));
}
```

`Promise.allSettled` (not `Promise.all`) is the deliberate choice behind test two: a rejected `send` must not throw out of `notifyOverdue`, and `allSettled` never rejects regardless of how many sends fail. `Promise.all` would make test two flaky-to-fail the moment the mock rejects.

